## 1. Introduction
As the development of MOF materials matures, research attention increasingly shifts to advancing MOF processing methods and improving their effective utilization. There is therefore significant focus on controlling the orientation of MOF crystals [1,2], as the majority of reported MOFs are non-cubic, possessing distinct physicochemical properties and functionality along different crystallographic directions [3–5]. Ubiquitous examples arise from MOF classes such as MIL-53 [6], MIL-68 [7] or NU-1000 [8], which bear 1-dimensional channels along the $c$ -axis (Fig. 1), along which molecular transport is maximized. Another example can be seen in optical waveguiding Ln-BTC microrods reported by Yang et al., whereby light propagating along the (001) direction of the microrods is circularly polarized due to the chiral helical arrangement of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions along this direction [9]. Besides molecular transport or light propagation, MOF properties such as conductivity can be highly anisotropic, as seen in the redox conductivity of NU-1000 which is 3500 times higher along the $c$ -axis than that through the $^{a,b}$ plane [10]. To fully harness their directional functionality, orientational control over MOFs is therefore essential.
In particular, since MOFs are typically prepared in bulk as loose microcrystalline powders, methods to directly orient and assemble such free-standing particles are particularly advantageous for fabrication of oriented composites or devices. Furthermore, for many MOF applications such as chemical separations, photonics or ion conduction in solid state electrolytes, densely packed, oriented MOF superstructures would be especially desirable. Selfassembly and orientation of particles hvae been shown to be important for their performance [11–13], and colloidal selfassembly of MOFs into ordered superstructures has been demonstrated by several groups [14–16], but is severely limited by the strict requirement of highly uniform particles for ordered packing, which presents a significant synthetic challenge for most MOFs. To develop a general approach for oriented MOF particle assembly, we turn to liquid crystal (LC) ordering as a novel, yet promising concept to form highly oriented anisotropic MOF superstructures. Liquid crystals refer to a state of matter with long-range order (within domains) which yet retains some degree of fluidity, and have been widely used for various optoelectronic applications, such as liquid crystal displays, thermometers [17], microlasers [18] and lasing resonators [19]. Besides anisotropic molecules, anisometric particles such as rod or discs, where one dimension is significantly different from the other two, may also assemble into liquid crystal phases (termed colloidal or mineral LCs) [20,21]. LCs based on molecules or particles dispersed in a solvent are described as lyotropic. Suspensions of rod-shaped particles can transition from an isotropic phase, where the rods are randomly oriented, to a nematic phase, where the rods show a preferred orientation. At sufficient packing densities, aligned packing decreases the total excluded volume around the rods and concomitantly increases the remaining free volume and thus translational entropy of the system when the rods are aligned, which outweighs the loss in orientational entropy [22]. Particle sedimentation is commonly carried out to increase the particle volume fraction towards the bottom of the sediment such that LC phase formation can occur.
## 2. Results and discussion
MIL-53- ${\cdot}\mathsf{N H}_{2}(\mathsf{A l})$ microneedles were synthesized in accordance to our previous report, using acetic acid as a modulator [31]. This gave rise to polydisperse particles with $\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{D}$ ratios of $10\pm5$ . MIL$68(\mathrm{In})_{\mathrm{long}}$ [32], NU-1000 [33] and MIL $-68(\mathrm{In})_{\mathrm{short}}$ [32] were also prepared according to the literature whereby their $\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{D}$ ratios are $6.8\pm0.9$ , $3.4\pm0.2$ and $1.2\pm0.2$ respectively (Fig. 2). It should also be noted that the effective $\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{D}$ ratios of the MOF particles may be lower than reported above, due to the presence of an electrical double layer on the surfaces of the particles and interparticle electrostatic repulsion [25,34]. The sedimentation and packing of the MOF particles was then investigated, whereby solvents of appropriate densities were selected to afford particle sedimentation within $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ or less.
The assemblies were fixed via photopolymerization and studied using scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM), which has been demonstrated as a method for imaging of fluorescent MOFs [9,35], to determine the particle arrangements.
## 2.1. MIL-53-NH2(Al)
MIL- ${\mathfrak{j}}3{\mathfrak{-N H}}_{2}({\mathsf{A l}})$ microneedles were dispersed in N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) solutions via sonication, giving 8 wt% MIL-53- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}(\mathsf{A l})/\mathsf{D M F}$ dispersions containing $10\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ photopolymerizable trimethylolpropane triacrylate (TMPTA) and 1 wt% diphenyl (2,4,6-trimethylbenzoyl)phosphine oxide photoinitiator (TPO). DMF was selected as the solvent as its high dielectric constant $\mathrm{\langle\ttE_{DMF}=36.7\rangle}$ led to well-dispersed suspensions of the polar MIL$53\mathrm{-NH}_{2}(\mathrm{Al})$ . The choice of polar solvent should also afford short inverse Debye screening lengths $\upkappa^{-1}$ [36], minimizing electrostatic repulsion between the MOF crystals to encourage their packing. Rectangular cross-section glass capillaries were utilized as cells for the particle assembly. Due to the strong tendency of MIL-53- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}(\mathsf{A l})$ particles to adhere to glass, thereby disrupting assembly, the inner surfaces of the glass capillaries were first silanized with trimethoxyoctadecylsilane (TMODS) to minimize particle adhesion. Subsequently, the cells were filled with the MOF suspensions via capillary action by dipping one end of the cell into the mixture. The glass capillaries were then flame sealed to avoid solvent evaporation, supported on glass slides for ease of handling and kept vertically upright, allowing the particles to sediment overnight, after which the sample was irradiated with $405~\mathrm{{nm}}$ light to polymerize the mixture and fix the particle assembly for microscopy studies. As MIL-53- ${\mathrm{.NH}}_{2}({\mathrm{Al}})$ is fluorescent [37], CLSM could be carried out on the resulting samples. We were pleased to observe that the MOF crystals could indeed assemble by LC ordering, as hypothesized. Moving from the top of the capillary towards the bottom, the increasing particle packing density led to increasing alignment of the MOFs along the capillary, forming an oriented nematic phase (Fig. 3b). Furthermore, the degree of orientation of the assemblies in the top, middle and bottom of the sedimentation layer was calculated by Fourier component analysis using the software Fiji ImageJ (Fig. 3b, right column), clearly depicting the increased älignment towards the bottom of the capillary.
We wished to study if sedimentation in an external E-field would further enhance the MOF alignment, particularly at the top of the sediment layer where particle packing density is low. To do so, similar capillary cells were used, which were placed above copper tape electrodes spaced $5~\mathrm{mm}$ apart on glass slides (Fig. 3a). Silver wires were affixed to the electrodes using silver paint and carbon tape and connected to an A.C. source. The capillary was held vertically upright and the MIL- $53\mathrm{-NH}_{2}(\mathrm{Al})$ crystals were assembled overnight whilst an alternating voltage was applied to the electrodes (1 MHz, $400\mathrm{V_{p-p}},$ , E-field $\mathrm{\sim14\V{\cdot}m m_{p-p.}^{-1}}$ ) respectively, giving an external E-field approximately parallel to gravity. Such high frequencies are commonly used to selectively allow dielectric polarization of the particles, and to avoid electroosmotic effects in the suspension [38,39], which tend to disrupt particle assembly, as ion migration is not able to follow such high E-field frequencies.
Importantly, this demonstrates the use of field-assisted liquid crystal assembly to generate highly aligned 3-dimensional MOF assemblies with long-ranged order, even for highly polydispersed particles.
## 2.2. MIL-68(In)long
We then moved to MIL- $-68(\mathrm{In)_{long}}$ particles, which possess a lower L/D ratio of 6.8 and repeated these experiments. As these particles did not adhere to the glass when dispersed in DMF, pretreatment of the glass capillaries with TMODS was unnecessary. The capillary cells were filled with $4\mathrm{wt\%}$ MIL-68/DMF dispersions containing $10\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ TMPTA and $1\mathrm{\Deltawt\%}$ TPO, flame sealed and held vertically upright for sedimentation. The higher density of MIL68(In) reduced the sedimentation time to four hours. After sedimentation, the sample was irradiated with $405\ \mathrm{nm}$ light to fix the MIL-68 alignment.
As MIL-68(In) is non-fluorescent, observations of the resulting assemblies were performed via SEM instead of CLSM. To do so, the glass capillaries of the polymerized samples were carefully cracked open for SEM observations. The low adhesion of MIL-68 (In) particles to glass allowed the release of a clean interface between the capillary glass wall and the MOF composite, minimizing disruption of the particle alignment.
Unlike with MIL- $53\mathrm{-NH}_{2}(\mathrm{Al})$ , the $\mathsf{M I L-}68(\mathrm{In})_{\mathrm{long}}$ particles generally show a low degree of directional order in the absence of an Efield. This is attributed to the lower $\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{D}$ ratio of MIL- $68(\mathrm{In})_{\mathrm{long}}$ relative to MIL-53 $\left.\phantom{\frac{1}{2}}\right.\mathrm{{NH}}_{2}(\mathsf{A l})$ , as well as the much faster sedimentation which arrests the particles in an aggregated disordered state. Nevertheless, as the particle packing density increases towards the bottom of the capillary, an increase in directional alignment along the gravitational field is observed (Fig. 4a). However, when the particles were assembled with an alternating potential applied to the electrodes (1 MHz, $400\mathrm{\:V_{p-p}},$ E-field $\mathrm{{\sim14\V{\cdot}m m_{p-p}^{-1}}}$ ), the resulting assemblies show excellent alignment of the MOF microrods into a nematic phase throughout the sediment layer, despite the rapid particle sedimentation. The degrees of orientation of the assemblies in glass capillaries were plotted as shown in Fig. 4b, clearly illustrating the increased directionality of the particles under an applied E-field.
We have seen that for both MIL-53- $\ensuremath{\mathsf{\mathrm{-NH}}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathsf{A l}})$ and MIL-68(In)long, gravity imposes a preference for vertical particle alignment, which is further enhanced when the applied E-field is parallel to gravity. However, to determine the flexibility of our approach, we aimed to see if highly oriented assemblies could also be obtained when the E-field and gravity are in competition, and perpendicular to each other. In this case, similar rectangular cross-section capillary cells were used as for previous experiments (Fig. 4c). However, opposite walls of the cells were coated with $\mathsf{A u/T i}$ external electrodes, and connected to an AC source via silver wires affixed with silver paint and carbon tape. This set-up afforded a more homogeneous E-field due to the presence of parallel facing electrodes compared with the earlier set-up, which is advantageous for avoidance of assemblydisrupting electroosmotic circulation. During sedimentation, the alternating potential (1 MHz, $200\mathrm{V_{p-p}},\sim56\mathrm{V}{\cdot}\mathrm{mm}_{\mathrm{p-p}}^{-1}$ ) was applied perpendicular to gravity and the capillary length. SEM observations show nematic phase formation whereby MIL-68(In) microrods are highly aligned parallel to the applied E-field, and alignment of the underlying particle layers can also be clearly seen when studying the edge of the sample (Fig. 4d, right most image). These observations demonstrate how the combination of E-fields with sedimentation processes allows flexibility in controlling the orientation of the resulting MOF assemblies.
## 2.3. NU-1000
Lastly, we aimed to study whether the application of an E-field during LC assembly could side-step the requirement of $\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{D}\geq4.7$ for nematic phase formation, by using NU-1000 microrods which have an $\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{D}$ ratio of 3.4. Besides their low $\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{D}$ ratio, the strong tendency of NU-1000 to adhere to the capillary glass walls further complicated their oriented alignment. We attribute this to the presence of unsaturated $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}$ nodes in NU-1000 [41] which may bind to Si-OH groups on the glass surfaces. This was partly mitigated by using TMODS-silanized glass capillaries and replacing DMF solvent with $o$ -difluorobenzene (DFB, $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{DFB}}=14.3\rangle$ . The density of DFB $\cdot1.16~\mathrm{g}.\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ , $25~^{\circ}C;$ is still sufficiently low to allow overnight MOF sedimentation. Given the lower polarity of DFB compared to DMF, a higher Debye screening length, $\upkappa^{-1}$ and thus increased interparticle electrostatic repulsions are expected, in line with what we observed previously with NU- $1000_{\mathrm{Si}}$ in bromobenzene [42]. Tetrabutylammonium bromide (TBABr) was therefore added to screen the interparticle electrostatic repulsions, to allow dense particle packing. The capillaries were filled with 4 wt% NU-1000/DFB dispersions containing 10 wt% TMPTA, 1 wt% TPO and $0.01\mathrm{\wt\%}$ TBABr, flame sealed and held vertically upright for overnight sedimentation. For sedimentation in the presence of an E-field parallel to gravity, the cells were supported on glass slides with copper electrodes spaced $5\mathrm{mm}$ apart, to which an alternating E-voltage $300\mathrm{kHz}$ , $400\mathrm{V_{p-p},}$ E-field: ${\sim}33\ \mathrm{V}{\cdot}\mathrm{mm}_{\mathrm{p}{-}\mathrm{p}}^{-1}$ was applied as this frequency afforded the best field alignment whilst still minimizing electroosmotic effects. Again, photopolymerization was carried out to fix the assembly.
The fluorescent nature of NU-1000, arising from the pyrenebased ligands, allowed CLSM studies to be carried out [42], which showed that in the absence of an E-field, the microrods were largely disordered across the sedimentation layer. However, a layer of vertically oriented particles can be seen adhering to the glass walls (Fig. 5a), which is reflected by the directionality plot showing an obvious peak at approximately $90^{\circ}$ (vertical direction). This arises from the vertical shear forces present during capillary filling of the NU-1000 suspension, coupled with the strong adhesion of NU-1000 particles to the capillary walls. As expected, E-field application markedly improved alignment from the top to the bottom of the sedimentation layer (Fig. 5b,c and Figure S2).
As the volume fraction of the particles increases, other phases such as smectic phases, whereby the particles assemble side-toside into orientationally ordered layers, can also form, if the particles possess sufficient length monodispersity (length standard deviation of $<18\%$ ), as uniform particle lengths allow the particles to assemble into layers more easily [43–45]. However, this was not observed for NU-1000, despite its low length polydispersity of $6\%$ - the adjacent rods are instead offset relative to each other. This staggering is attributed to the electrostatic interparticle repulsion between the ends of side-by-side adjacent particles, arising from E-field induced particle dipoles, in line with molecular dynamics simulations by Rotunno et al [46] as well as experimental observations by Kuijk et al. on silica microrods in E-fields [40].
To view the MOFs in the deeper underlying layers within the assembly, samples were cracked open for SEM analysis. The strong adhesion of the MOF/polymer composite to the glass led to the crack propagating within the composite matrix, disrupting the particle arrangement. Nevertheless, SEM analysis of the rough crack interface shows that the NU-1000 particles are still generally aligned (Figure S3).
Sedimentation was also carried out using E-fields perpendicular to gravity, whereby narrower capillaries (refer to ESI for details) with Ti/Au electrodes along the capillary length were used (Fig. 4c). After overnight sedimentation (18 h) under a sinusoidal potential $300\mathrm{kHz}$ , $200\mathsf{V}_{\mathsf{p-p}},$ , E-field $\sim153\mathrm{V}{\cdot}\mathrm{mm}_{\mathrm{p}{\cdot}\mathrm{p}}^{-1}$ ) applied perpendicular to the capillary length, the sample was fixed by photopolymerization for further observations. An approximately 1 cm high yellow layer of sedimented NU-1000 microrods could be clearly observed (Fig. 6a). CLSM tiled scanning along the entire length of the MOF assembly showed that throughout most of the yellow layer (approx $0.8~\mathrm{cm}^{\cdot}$ ), the rods were oriented along the applied E-field, except near the meniscus, due to electroosmotic circulation and Marangoni effects at the meniscus (Figure S4) as well as adhesion of vertically oriented particles to the glass walls. Z-stacked CLSM of the assembly edges along the inner glass capillary wall showed that the MOF layers beneath the top layer were aligned perpendicular to the capillary length (Fig. 1d) and SEM analysis of opened capillary samples also shows the alignment of the NU-1000 microrods at a crack interface within the composite, across particle layers of different depths (Fig. 6e), indicating a three-dimensional oriented superstructure.
## 2.4. MIL-68(In)short
As the oriented assembly of NU-1000 was complicated by their strong adhesion to the capillary glass, shorter microrods of MIL-68 (In) (referred to as MIL $-68(\mathrm{In})_{\mathrm{short}},$ to distinguish them from the longer MIL-68(In) particles) with aspect ratio of 1.2 were prepared to better study the effect of a lower aspect ratio and to observe the impact of E-fields on particles with aspect ratios close to 1. Experiments were carried with the same conditions as analogous experiments for MIL- $68(\mathrm{In})_{\mathrm{long}},$ , where the capillary cells were filled with 4 wt% MIL-68/DMF dispersions containing $10\mathrm{wt\%}$ TMPTA and 1 wt $\%$ TPO. SEM images of opened capillary samples showed that in the absence of an E-field, the particles display no obvious preferred orientation (Fig. 7a), but in the presence of both a vertical E-field (1 MHz, $400\mathrm{V_{p-p}},$ , E-field $\mathrm{\sim14\V{\cdot}m m_{p-p}^{-1},}$ approximately parallel to gravity) and a horizontal E-field (1 MHz, $200\mathrm{V_{p-p}},\sim56\mathrm{V}{\cdot}\mathrm{mm}_{\mathrm{p-p}}^{-1}$ , perpendicular to gravity), clear particle alignment could be observed (Fig. 7b,c). The alignment of lower layers of underlying particles could also be clearly observed in Fig. 7d, whilst a low magnification SEM image shows the long-ranged order of the assembly (Fig. 7e). It is worth noting that the oriented assembly of these particles is remarkable, given that the aspect ratio of these particles is close to 1, which serves to underline the impact of an external E-field for ordered particle assembly.
## 3. Conclusion
We have demonstrated a general approach for the macroscale assembly and long-ranged orientation control of a variety of freestanding MOF particles. Taking NU-1000, MIL-68(In) and MIL-53- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}(\mathsf{A l})$ as examples of particles with $\mathsf{L}/\mathsf{D}$ ratios ranging from 1.2 to 10, particle LC phases were formed under the combined application of electric and gravitational fields. Through TMPTA photopolymerization, the orientation of the MOF assemblies could be fixed for subsequent studies. Importantly, this approach overcomes the traditional limitations of colloidal and nematic assembly, being applicable not just to particles with low $\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{D}$ ratios $(<4.7)$ , but also particles with high polydispersity, thereby representing a breakthrough strategy for the control and assembly of MOF particles. The insights obtained in this work are widely applicable not just to MOFs, but also to other particles, paving the way for the development of next generation particle composites and devices.
